# postui-dmesg — caps.decl
#
# Wave: postui v1 reference apps
# Milestone: M1 (scaffold + KIND_DMESG poll)
# Doc:  paideia-os/postui/docs/design.md §3.3 (widget mix + data
#       source for /bin/dmesg); paideia-os/postui/design/architecture.md
#       §5.1 (error-band allocation table).
#
# postui-dmesg is the klog tail viewer that consumes the postui widget
# library at exec time. Its cap footprint at M1-001 covers every
# substrate the poll + eventual render paths will touch — the scaffold
# declares the full set up front so the M2 render-landing and M4
# semantic-pipe-emit landings do not need to reopen the manifest for
# a re-sign.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# KIND_TUI_CANVAS — the render substrate (postui/docs/design.md §2.1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# postui-dmesg holds one canvas cap per rendering surface (single
# canvas for the fullscreen dmesg view at M2; the M3 tabs-per-severity
# view stays inside one canvas via sub-region draws rather than a
# compositor split — postui/docs/design.md §3.3 tabs treatment).
#
# Rights layout (mirrors R_TTY_* discipline; see postui/docs/design.md
# §2.1 "Rights" table and postui-top/caps.decl for the same rationale):
#
#   R_TUI_PRESENT   0x002   diff + emit a frame (the write analogue)
#   R_TUI_INVOKE    0x008   query ops (rows, cols, id, tty_id)
#   R_TUI_RESIZE    0x010   TUI_OP_RESIZE / TUI_OP_CLEAR
#   R_TUI_REVOKE    0x020   teardown the row
#   R_TUI_MINT      0x200   derive a canvas from a KIND_MEMORY parent
#   R_TUI_OBSERVE   0x400   debug printer
#
# At M1-001 postui-dmesg's poll module (src/klog_poll.pdx) does NOT
# touch the canvas — the KIND_DMESG poll runs before any render. The
# mint and present rights are declared HERE so the M1-005 render
# landing (which introduces terminal_init + frame_render +
# frame_present through postui) does not need a caps.decl churn:
#
#   * mint    — postui-dmesg mints its top-level canvas at Main::main
#               startup via sys_cap_derive against a KIND_MEMORY parent
#               (M1-005 landing).
#   * present — every frame_render tail invokes TUI_OP_PRESENT via
#               postui's tcc_present wrapper (M2-onwards render path).
#
# R_TUI_INVOKE / R_TUI_RESIZE / R_TUI_REVOKE are DEFERRED (declared at
# M1-005 when the render path first exercises them); adding them at
# this landing would over-declare authority the M1-001 poll surface
# never uses.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# KIND_TTY — the input + physical-sink substrate (postui/docs/design.md
# §2.5)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two independent uses (mirror postui / postui-top / shell's own
# caps.decl):
#
#   * write — the canvas is provably wired to a live KIND_TTY at mint
#     time (postui/docs/design.md §2.1: the row's tty_slot is validated
#     via tty_tail_valid at cap_handler_tui_canvas mint). TUI_OP_PRESENT
#     emits its ANSI byte stream through the tty_slot; the write right
#     is transitively required at postui-dmesg mint time even though
#     postui-dmesg code never issues TTY_OP_WRITE itself.
#
#   * read — the primary input path once postui.M4-007 lands (Terminal::
#     terminal_read_input probes KIND_TTY raw-mode). The M2 TextInput
#     filter widget consumes this. Declared here so the M2 filter
#     landing does not need to widen the manifest.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# KIND_MEMORY — the canvas backing store (postui/docs/design.md §2.1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# KIND_TUI_CANVAS derives over KIND_MEMORY (= KIND_PAGE, 4).
# postui-dmesg mints a KIND_MEMORY region sized 2 * rows * cols *
# CELL_BYTES at M1-005 startup and hands it to sys_cap_derive
# (KIND_TUI_CANVAS, ...); the derived canvas cap is what postui
# subsequently invokes for every PRESENT. The mint right must be
# held; the derive-then-drop pattern means we do NOT hold KIND_MEMORY
# authority after the derive completes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# KIND_DMESG — the klog tail substrate (paideia-os
# src/kernel/core/cap/kind_dmesg.pdx, logging-m9-001 #708)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The forward-looking capability the poll module treats as its data
# source. Kernel body defines two ops:
#
#   * OP_READ_TAIL (op_code 0)  — RIGHT_READ; copies up to `len` bytes
#     of the klog ring tail into a caller-owned buffer. Returns bytes
#     copied in rax.
#   * OP_STAT      (op_code 1)  — RIGHT_READ; returns
#     (head_lo32 << 32) | KLOG_RING_SIZE so a reader can track new
#     bytes since its last poll.
#
# At M1-001 the poll module CANNOT actually invoke KIND_DMESG through
# sys_cap_invoke because R18/R19 has no per-TCB cap-slot table for
# userspace processes yet — kind_dmesg.pdx's own comment (see
# `Cap descriptor: target_ptr = kernel VA of a caller-owned scratch
# buffer`) and sys_dmesg.pdx's access-control note ("R18 has no
# per-TCB cap-holder machinery yet — deferred to R19+ userspace-daemon
# substrate, so at this landing sys_dmesg is unrestricted") both
# reflect the same reality: the KIND_DMESG cap is minted at boot and
# available for future gating, but the reachable userspace path today
# is the sysno 13 (sys_dmesg) syscall, which wraps klog_ring_read_tail
# with the same semantics OP_READ_TAIL exposes.
#
# The M1-001 poll module therefore uses sys_dmesg (sysno 13) directly
# — see the syscalls block below and src/klog_poll.pdx's
# kp_sys_dmesg wrapper. KIND_DMESG(read) is declared HERE so the
# M2+ landing that switches from the sysno-13 fallback to
# sys_cap_invoke (once TCB cap-slot machinery lands) does not need a
# caps.decl churn — the manifest already claims the authority.
#
# Rights layout (see kind_dmesg.pdx SECTION 3 "Cap descriptor"):
#
#   RIGHT_READ      0x001   OP_READ_TAIL + OP_STAT both require this
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
requires:
- KIND_TUI_CANVAS(mint, present)
- KIND_TTY(write, read)
- KIND_MEMORY(mint, revoke)
- KIND_DMESG(read)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Syscall dependencies (informational; not consumed by the InitCap
# sidecar — the syscall gate is a kernel-side dispatch decision, not a
# per-process cap check at M1). Enumerated here so downstream tooling
# (release lint, dependency-audit) that walks the tree can trace
# postui-dmesg's SC+ surface without reading every .pdx source.
#
# The M1-001 poll path uses exactly two syscalls beyond what postui's
# own render path already declares:
#
#   * sysno 13 — sys_dmesg(buf, len) -> bytes copied in [0, len]
#                Kernel-to-userspace copy of the klog ring tail
#                (logging-m9-002 #709; body at src/kernel/core/syscall/
#                handlers/sys_dmesg.pdx wrapping klog_ring_read_tail).
#                Idempotent-peek POSIX-style contract: every call
#                returns the current ring tail bytes; no consume-cursor
#                is maintained kernel-side (the M2+ streaming landing
#                will track head via KIND_DMESG OP_STAT once cap-slot
#                machinery is reachable). Dispatch shim caps `len` at
#                DISPATCH_IO_MAX (4096 bytes) — see dispatch.pdx
#                dispatch_dmesg_len_ok.
#   * sysno 66 — sys_clock_read_ns() -> u64 ns
#                Monotonic ns-since-boot counter. KlogPoll uses it to
#                gate the fixed-interval poll_tick refresh (converting
#                interval_ticks to a compatible ns-domain deadline via
#                shift, per src/klog_poll.pdx §Tick semantics — same
#                2^24-ns tick shape postui-top adopted).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
syscalls:
- sys_dmesg         @ 13
- sys_clock_read_ns @ 66

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Declared output schemas
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# postui-dmesg emits one first-class semantic-pipe schema at M4-004:
#
#   * DmesgLineView@0.1 — one record per parsed klog line, mirroring
#                         the internal log_line struct (severity u64,
#                         timestamp_ns u64, text_ptr u64, text_len u64
#                         = 32 wire bytes; the +32..+255 reserved
#                         growth region stays in-process, not on the
#                         wire). Emitted through the KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT
#                         postui-dmesg binds at Main::main startup
#                         (M2-005 wire-in). Every downstream scraper
#                         (e.g. a monitoring daemon that pulls
#                         DmesgLineView via `dmesg | scraper`) reads
#                         the same 32-byte-BLAKE3 schema hash under
#                         the libpdx-semantic-pipe v1 contract.
#
# At M1-001 the emitter path is scaffolding only (klog_poll.pdx
# populates the in-memory line ring; no envelope encoder runs). The
# schema name is declared here so the M4-004 landing does not need to
# introduce it and the downstream dependency-audit tree already sees
# postui-dmesg as a DmesgLineView producer.
declares_output_schemas:
- DmesgLineView@0.1
